PMID- 36081613
OWN - NLM
STAT- PubMed-not-MEDLINE
LR  - 20220913
IS  - 2641-3590 (Print)
IS  - 2641-3604 (Electronic)
IS  - 2641-3590 (Linking)
VI  - 2019
DP  - 2019 May
TI  - Deep Transfer Learning Across Cancer Registries for Information Extraction from 
      Pathology Reports.
LID - 10.1109/bhi.2019.8834586 [doi]
AB  - Automated text information extraction from cancer pathology reports is an active 
      area of research to support national cancer surveillance. A well-known challenge 
      is how to develop information extraction tools with robust performance across 
      cancer registries. In this study we investigated whether transfer learning (TL) 
      with a convolutional neural network (CNN) can facilitate cross-registry knowledge 
      sharing. Specifically, we performed a series of experiments to determine whether 
      a CNN trained with single-registry data is capable of transferring knowledge to 
      another registry or whether developing a cross-registry knowledge database 
      produces a more effective and generalizable model. Using data from two cancer 
      registries and primary tumor site and topography as the information extraction 
      task of interest, our study showed that TL results in 6.90% and 17.22% 
      improvement of classification macro F-score over the baseline single-registry 
      models. Detailed analysis illustrated that the observed improvement is evident in 
      the low prevalence classes.
