DP  - 2002
TI  - A successful technique for removing names in pathology reports using an augmented 
      search and replace method.
PG  - 777-81
AB  - The ability to access large amounts of de-identified clinical data would 
      facilitate epidemiologic and retrospective research. Previously described 
      de-identification methods require knowledge of natural language processing or 
      have not been made available to the public. We take advantage of the fact that 
      the vast majority of proper names in pathology reports occur in pairs. In rare 
      cases where one proper name is by itself, it is preceded or followed by an affix 
      that identifies it as a proper name (Mrs., Dr., PhD). We created a tool based on 
      this observation using substitution methods that was easy to implement and was 
      largely based on publicly available data sources. We compiled a Clinical and 
      Common Usage Word (CCUW) list as well as a fairly comprehensive proper name list. 
      Despite the large overlap between these two lists, we were able to refine our 
      methods to achieve accuracy similar to previous attempts at de-identification. 
      Our method found 98.7% of 231 proper names in the narrative sections of pathology 
      reports. Three single proper names were missed out of 1001 pathology reports 
      (0.3%, no first name/last name pairs). It is unlikely that identification could 
      be implied from this information. We will continue to refine our methods, 
      specifically working to improve the quality of our CCUW and proper name lists to 
      obtain higher levels of accuracy.
